{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Quick now supports cross-account access for Amazon Athena data sources - AWS","context":"Amazon Quick has introduced cross-account access for Amazon Athena data sources. This feature allows users to query Athena data in different AWS accounts using IAM role chaining. The query costs will be billed to the account where the data is located. Administrators can create an Athena data source in Quick by specifying a RunAsRole in the Quick account and a ConsumerAccountRoleArn in the target account. Quick uses a role chaining mechanism to assume the RunAsRole and then chain into the consumer account role to execute queries. This launch supports multiple roles per consumer account, enabling fine-grained access segregation across teams within a single account. The feature is available in all supported Amazon Quick regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/amazon-quick-athena/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"The AWS MCP Server now supports cross-account and cross-role access - AWS","context":"AWS announced the addition of cross-account and cross-role access support for the AWS Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server, which is part of the Agent Toolkit for AWS. This feature enables developers using AI coding agents like Kiro, Claude Code, or Codex to work across multiple AWS accounts and IAM roles within a single session without the need for restarts. Previously, switching profiles necessitated stopping the AI coding session, updating local AWS credentials, and restarting the MCP server for every account change. Now, AI agents using the AWS MCP Server can specify a profile on each command, allowing users to switch between accounts and roles seamlessly. Cross-account access facilitates faster development in multi-account environments, enabling tasks such as querying CloudWatch logs across production and staging accounts or updating a Lambda configuration in one account and adjusting an S3 bucket policy in another, all within the same conversation. The AWS MCP Server is currently available in the US East (N. Virginia) and Europe (Frankfurt) Regions. To get started, refer to the Multi-profile support section in the Agent Toolkit for AWS user guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/aws-mcp-server/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"ingesting-into-data-lake","skill_description":"Import data into the AWS data lake from S3 files, local uploads, JDBC databases (Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, RDS, Aurora), Amazon Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery, DynamoDB, or existing Glue catalog tables (migration). Default target is S3 Tables; standard Iceberg on a general purpose bucket is supported where S3 Tables is not adopted. Handles one-time loads, recurring pipelines, migrations. Triggers on: import data, load data, ingest, sync database, migrate table, move data to AWS, set up pipeline, ETL, pull from Snowflake, query BigQuery into S3, export DynamoDB, CTAS, convert to Iceberg. Do NOT use for setting up or troubleshooting Glue connections (use connecting-to-data-source), creating empty tables (use creating-data-lake-table), running queries (use querying-data-lake), finding tables by fuzzy name (use finding-data-lake-assets), catalog audit (use exploring-data-catalog), or SaaS platforms like Salesforce, ServiceNow, SAP, MongoDB, Kafka.\n\n\nServices: glue, s3, s3tables, athena, dynamodb\nTasks: deploy, migrate\nPersona: developer, data-engineer\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"ingesting-into-data-lake"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"managing-data-catalog","skill_description":"Writes and curates business metadata in an AWS data catalog: the Amazon SageMaker Catalog (Amazon DataZone), or the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Detects which catalog platform an account uses before writing. Triggers on: add a description to a table, document a dataset, write a README, set an owner, add column comments, tag with a glossary term, create a business glossary, create a metadata form, publish an asset, create a data product, run the data source crawl, delete a catalog asset, attach a conventions document to a table as a catalog skill asset, which assets in my project are missing descriptions or owners and what to fix first, which catalog does this account use and what can I do with it. Not for: inventorying or listing tables (use exploring-data-catalog), locating one table (use finding-data-lake-assets), counting gaps domain-wide with SQL over the exported metadata snapshot (use querying-aws-sagemaker-catalog), or granting or revoking access (use managing-data-asset-access)\n\n\nServices: datazone, glue\nTasks: deploy, audit\nPersona: data-engineer, developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-data-catalog"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"managing-data-asset-access","skill_description":"Grants, requests, audits and removes access to data assets in an AWS data catalog (the Amazon SageMaker Catalog supported by Amazon DataZone) through catalog subscriptions over published listings. Covers requesting a subscription, approving or rejecting requests with a recorded decision, sharing a listing with a project, revoking one or many subscriptions, and confirming grant settlement. Triggers on: give another team access to a table, request access to a dataset, approve or reject an access request, share a listing with a project, who has access to this table, revoke access, bulk revoke, is the access actually gone, subscription grant status. Not for: IAM roles, policies or STS (use aws-iam), S3 bucket policies (use securing-s3-buckets), Clean Rooms permissions (use aws-cleanrooms), or writing catalog metadata, glossaries and forms, publishing listings, or retiring or deleting an asset that other teams are subscribed to and the order of operations that needs (use managing-data-catalog)\n\n\nServices: datazone\nTasks: deploy, audit\nPersona: data-engineer, developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-data-asset-access"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS Forecast docs on RoleArn account requirements for CreateDatasetImportJob"}
